Article 31

( 1 ) The Regency Government is obligated and responsible to conduct prevention and handling of cases of children who are victims of neglect, violence, discrimination, exploitation, child trafficking, abuse, narcotics, psychotropics, and other addictive substances, and/or pornography.

( 2 ) The responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. respecting, fulfilling, and protecting children's rights;
b. conducting socialization about the adverse effects of neglect, violence, discrimination, exploitation, child trafficking, abuse, narcotics, psychotropics, and other addictive substances, and/or pornography;
c. making policies and/or policy changes regarding child protection;
d. providing assistance;
e. conducting training on handling cases of neglect, violence, discrimination, exploitation, child trafficking, abuse, narcotics, psychotropics, and other addictive substances, and/or pornography against children for stakeholders;
f. assisting in the social rehabilitation and social reintegration process;
g. providing temporary shelters for children; and
h. conducting coordination and cooperation with stakeholders related to handling child cases.

Article 73

Prevention as referred to in Article 70 shall be carried out by means of:

a. involving the Community in implementing Special Protection for Children;
b. increasing understanding related to kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking of Children;
c. establishing bilateral and multilateral cooperation, both national and international;
d. improving family resilience to prevent Children from kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking; and
e. increasing the responsibility of the Community, business world, and mass media to protect Children from kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking.

Article 131

(1) The community may participate in organizing the protection of women and the protection of children.

(2) Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be implemented in the context of prevention, handling and empowerment, and fulfillment of children's rights.

(3) The role of the community in organizing child protection as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:

a. providing information through socialization and education regarding children's rights and laws and regulations concerning children;
b. providing input in the formulation of policies related to child protection;
c. reporting to the competent authorities if violations of children's rights occur;
d. playing an active role in the rehabilitation and social reintegration process for children;
e. conducting monitoring, supervision and being responsible for the organization of child protection;
f. providing facilities and infrastructure and creating a conducive atmosphere for children's growth and development;
g. establishing a Regency regional coordination forum;
h. playing an active role by eliminating negative labeling of Children; and
i. providing space for children to participate and express their opinions.
(4) The role of community organizations and educational institutions as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out by taking necessary steps in accordance with their respective duties, functions, and authorities to assist in organizing child protection and providing information.
(5) The role of mass media as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out through the dissemination of information and educational materials that are beneficial from the social, cultural, educational, religious and child health aspects by taking into account the best interests of Children.
(6) The role of the business sector as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out through:
a. corporate policies with a child perspective;
b. products intended for children must be safe for children; and
c. contributing to the fulfillment of children's rights through corporate social responsibility.

Article 47

Involvement of various companies, labor unions, non-governmental organizations, and the Community in the elimination of economic and/or sexual exploitation of Children as referred to in Article 44 letter c shall be carried out through:

a. formulation of policies on the elimination of economic and/or sexual exploitation of Children in their environment;
b. cooperation to prevent Children from being economically and/or sexually exploited;
c. campaigns for the elimination of economic and/or sexual exploitation of Children;
d. increased supervision to prevent Children from being economically and/or sexually exploited;
e. corporate support for the elimination of exploitation of Children through corporate social responsibility; and
f. reporting to the competent authorities if there is economic and/or sexual exploitation of Children.

Article 51

Prevention efforts as referred to in Article 49 shall be carried out through:

a. communication, information, and education about the dangers to Children if involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances;
b. increasing the role of parents, families, the Community, educators, education personnel, religious leaders, customary leaders, Community leaders in supporting the Social Reintegration process of Children Who Become Victims of Narcotics, Alcohol, Psychotropic, and Other Addictive Substance Abuse;
c. providing understanding and awareness to Children regarding the dangers of smoking;
d. providing understanding and awareness to Children about the dangers of Children being involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances;
e. involving Children as peers in order to provide understanding and change of mindset about the dangers of Children being involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances; and
f. inclusion of anti-narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance teaching materials in education units.

Article 60

(1) If necessary, the District Government and the Community may undertake prevention efforts so that Children do not become victims of pornography.

(2) Prevention efforts by the District Government as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out through:

a. formulation and implementation of policies related to regional action plans on the prevention and handling of pornography;
b. optimization of the role of the Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Pornography;
c. capacity building of the Regional Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Pornography;
d. formulation and dissemination of communication, information, and education materials related to the prevention of pornography in schools and the Community;
e. guidance to the Community not to engage in activities that lead to pornography; and
f. guidance to Regional Agencies, non-governmental organizations, and the Community to enhance efforts in the prevention and handling of pornography.
(3) Prevention efforts by the Community so that Children do not become Victims of Pornography through:
a. dissemination of communication, information, and education materials related to the prevention of pornography in the surrounding environment;
b. strengthening the surrounding environment not to engage in activities that lead to pornography; and
c. enhancing prevention and handling efforts of pornography in the surrounding environment.
