c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are economically and/or sexually exploited;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children who are victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. children who are victims of physical and/or mental violence;
j. children who are victims of sexual crimes;
k. children who are victims of terrorism networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. children who are victims of abuse and neglect; n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
(2) Special Protection for Children is carried out through efforts to:
a. provide prompt handling, including physical, psychological, and social treatment and/or rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. provide psychosocial assistance from treatment to recovery;
c. provide social assistance for Children from underprivileged families; and
d. provide protection and assistance in every judicial process.
(3) Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided at the technical implementing units of ministries/agencies, PD, and/or regional technical implementing units that have been established with reference to established service standards.
(4) Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented quickly, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner.

Article 81

Special Protection for Child Victims of Sexual Crimes as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (1) letter j shall be carried out through:

a. education on reproductive health, religious values, and moral values;
b. Social Rehabilitation;
c. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery; and
d. provision of protection and assistance at every level of examination from investigation, prosecution, to examination in court hearings.

Article 80

(1) Dissemination and socialization of the provisions of laws and regulations that protect Child victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 74 shall be carried out by the Regency Government.

(2) Monitoring and reporting as referred to in Article 78 shall be carried out by the Regency Government.

(3) Implementation of Special Protection as referred to in Article 79 shall be carried out by:

a. the Regency Government through Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in the field of education;
b. educators;
c. educational personnel;
d. families; and/or
e. the Community, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 73

Prevention as referred to in Article 70 shall be carried out by means of:

a. involving the Community in implementing Special Protection for Children;
b. increasing understanding related to kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking of Children;
c. establishing bilateral and multilateral cooperation, both national and international;
d. improving family resilience to prevent Children from kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking; and
e. increasing the responsibility of the Community, business world, and mass media to protect Children from kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking.

Article 24

(1) In order to implement Child Protection, the Regency Government is obligated to carry out activities:

a. formulation of policies, programs, and regional development activities that incorporate children's rights perspectives as set forth in regional development planning documents;
b. formulation of norms, standard procedures and assessment criteria for the fulfillment and protection of children's rights that refer to the norms, standards, procedures and criteria established by the Government;
c. coordination of child-friendly Regency development implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. facilitation of the implementation of regional children's forums;
e. enhancement of community participation in the implementation and protection of children's rights;
f. coordination and cooperation in the fulfillment and protection of Children;
g. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of programs for the fulfillment and protection of Children;
h. guidance of child protection institutions; and i. providing special protection for Children.
(2) In addition to the activities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regency Government may carry out supervision and facilitation of child advocacy institutions in the Regency that are involved in providing assistance to Children requiring special handling.
(3) Supervision and facilitation as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(4) Provision of facilitation as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be adjusted to the regional financial capacity.

